  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Chlorine oxides,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>ClO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;are highly reactive oxidising agents and tend to explode.</p><p>Chemical reactivity of an element can be determined by its reaction with oxygen and halogens.</p>",
